{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of upward momentum: The model consistently fails to capture the magnitude of price spikes (e.g., the rise from ~85 to ~96 in Jan 2022), often predicting a flat or slightly declining trend while the market rallies sharply.",
    "Reduce forecast horizon bias: The model exhibits a 'flat-lining' behavior, predicting a near-constant price for the full 30-day horizon regardless of recent volatility; forecasts must be adjusted to reflect the actual volatility and trend direction observed in the last 18 days.",
    "Incorporate sentiment lag: Negative sentiment scores (-0.50) in the history do not trigger immediate downward corrections in the model; instead, the model often ignores these signals and maintains high baseline prices, leading to overestimation during dips.",
    "Adjust for mean reversion timing: The model predicts a slow, linear drift toward a mean that is too high; in reality, prices revert to lower levels faster after spikes, requiring a steeper downward correction in the first 5-10 days of the forecast window.",
    "Calibrate for post-spike overshoot: After a price spike (e.g., >88), the model fails to anticipate the subsequent volatility and potential drop, maintaining an artificially high floor; forecasts should be reduced by 5-10% immediately following any 3-day price increase >5%."
  ]
}
